=0\\) (\\(slope=-1\\)). Hence \\(L_1\\) is sunny.  \n- \\(L_2\\) is vertical, thus parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n- \\(L_3\\) is horizontal, thus parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n\nThe three lines are clearly distinct.\n\n---\n\n#### Point\u2011by\u2011point verification  \n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line(s)? | Check |\n|-----------------|------------------------|-------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_1\\) (since \\(1=1\\)) ; also on \\(L_2\\) and \\(L_3\\) | \\(1=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_1\\) (since \\(2=2\\)) | \\(2=2\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_2\\) (since \\(x=1\\)) | \\(x=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_2\\) (since \\(x=1\\)) | \\(x=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_3\\) (since \\(y=1\\)) | \\(y=1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_3\\) (since \\(y=1\\)) | \\(y=1\\) \u2713 |\n\nEvery point of \\(T_3\\) belongs to at least one of the three lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Counting sunny lines  \n\nAmong \\(\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\\) only \\(L_1\\) is sunny; \\(L_2\\) and \\(L_3\\) are non\u2011sunny. Hence the configuration contains exactly \\(k=1\\) sunny line.\n\n---\n\nSince the construction satisfies both required conditions, we have proved that for \\(n=3\\) a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists. \u220e"
