            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the condition “\\(a,b\\) positive integers with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)” yields the set  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must exhibit three distinct lines, all **sunny** (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)), such that every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Possible slopes between points of \\(T_3\\)\n\nFor any two distinct points of \\(T_3\\) compute the slope:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & \\text{slope} \\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1 \\\\\n(1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12 \\\\\n(1,3)-(2,1) & -2 \\\\\n(2,2)-(3,1) & -1 \\\\\n\\text{any pair sharing }x\\text{-coordinate} & \\infty \\\\\n\\text{any pair sharing }y\\text{-coordinate} & 0\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus the only slopes that are **not** forbidden (i.e. not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\)) are \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\). Consequently a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_3\\); a third point would force the slope to be one of the forbidden values.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Pairing the points\n\nChoose three disjoint pairs whose connecting lines have the admissible slopes:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_1 &= \\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}, &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\nP_2 &= \\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}, &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\nP_3 &= \\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}, &&\\text{slope }-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese three pairs cover all six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Equations of the three lines  \n\n* **Line \\(L_1\\)** through \\(P_1\\):  \n  \\[\n  y-1 = 1\\,(x-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad L_1:\\; y = x .\n  \\]\n\n* **Line \\(L_2\\)** through \\(P_2\\):  \n  \\[\n  y-2 = -\\frac12\\,(x-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad L_2:\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac52 .\n  \\]\n\n* **Line \\(L_3\\)** through \\(P_3\\):  \n  \\[\n  y-3 = -2\\,(x-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad L_3:\\; y = -2x + 5 .\n  \\]\n\nThe slopes \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\) are distinct, so the lines are distinct. None of these slopes equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\); hence each line is sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Verification that every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on a line  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{Point }(a,b) & \\text{Line containing it} & \\text{Check} \\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_1 & 1 = 1 \\;\\checkmark \\\\\n(2,2) & L_1 & 2 = 2 \\;\\checkmark \\\\\n(1,2) & L_2 & 2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac52 = 2 \\;\\checkmark \\\\\n(3,1) & L_2 & 1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac52 = 1 \\;\\checkmark \\\\\n(1,3) & L_3 & 3 = -2\\cdot1 + 5 = 3 \\;\\checkmark \\\\\n(2,1) & L_3 & 1 = -2\\cdot2 + 5 = 1 \\;\\checkmark\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAll six points are covered.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited three distinct sunny lines that together contain every point required for \\(n=3\\). Therefore the value  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=3}\n\\]\n\nis attainable. Hence for \\(n=3\\) the integer \\(k=3\\) satisfies the conditions of the problem.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
