h \\(P\\).*  \n   The three lines determined by the pairs of points of \\(P\\) are respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{pair} & \\text{type of line} \\\\ \\hline\n   (n-1,1)-(n-1,2) & \\text{vertical} \\\\\n   (n-1,1)-(n,1)   & \\text{horizontal} \\\\\n   (n-1,2)-(n,1)   & \\text{parallel to }x+y=0\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   All three types are **non\u2011sunny**.  Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(P\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 contradiction.*  \n   We have exactly two sunny lines but three points of \\(P\\) each require its own sunny line.  This is impossible.  Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   Assume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 all non\u2011sunny lines may be taken vertical.*  \n   The same replacement argument as in the case \\(k=2\\) shows that we may assume the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 the remaining triangular region.*  \n   After these vertical lines are placed, the points of \\(T_n\\) that are not yet covered form the right\u2011hand triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   This set contains exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points (it is a copy of \\(T_k\\) shifted to the right).\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 one\u2011to\u2011one correspondence with the leftmost column.*  \n   The leftmost column of \\(S_k\\) is \\(a=n-k+1\\) and consists of the \\(k\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\{(n-k+1,b)\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A sunny line cannot be vertical, therefore it can meet this column in **at most one** point.  Since we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each point of \\(C\\) must lie on a different sunny line.  Denote by \\(L_i\\) the sunny line that c