_n\\); therefore the replacement does not decrease the set of covered points.  Repeating this for every non‑sunny line we obtain a new configuration with the same two sunny lines, the same total number of lines, and all \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines vertical.\n\n   *Step 2 – forced vertical columns.*  \n   After the reduction we may assume the vertical lines are \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  These lines cover every point of the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\).  The only points of \\(T_n\\) that may remain uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   P=\\{(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 3 – nature of lines through \\(P\\).*  \n   The three lines determined by the pairs of points of \\(P\\) are respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{pair} & \\text{type of line} \\\\ \\hline\n   (n-1,1)-(n-1,2) & \\text{vertical} \\\\\n   (n-1,1)-(n,1)   & \\text{horizontal} \\\\\n   (n-1,2)-(n,1)   & \\text{parallel to }x+y=0\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   All three types are **non‑sunny**.  Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(P\\).\n\n   *Step 4 – contradiction.*  \n   We have exactly two sunny lines but three points of \\(P\\) each require its own sunny line.  This is impossible.  Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   Assume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n   *Step 1 – all non‑sunny lines may be taken vertical.*  \n   The same replacement argument as in the case \\(k=2\\) shows that we may assume the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining triangular region.*  \n   After these vertical lines are placed, the points of \\(T_n\\) that are not yet covered form the right‑hand triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   This set contains exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points (it is a copy of \\(T_k\\) shifted to the right).\n\n   *Step 3 – one‑to‑one correspondence with the leftmost column.*  \n   The leftmost column of \\(S_k\\) is \\(a=n-k+1\\) and consists of the \\(k\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\{(n-k+1,b)\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A sunny line cannot be vertical, therefore it can meet this column in **at most one** point.  Since we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each point of \\(C\\) must lie on a different sunny line.  Denote by \\(L_i\\) the sunny line that contains the point \\((n-k+1,i)\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).\n\n   *Step 4 – the rightmost column forces a restriction.*  \n   The rightmost column of \\(S_k\\) consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\).  For a line \\(L_i\\) to contain this point we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{slope}(L_i)=\\frac{1-i}{k-1}.\n   \\]\n\n   This slope is forbidden precisely when \\(i=1\\) (slope \\(0\\)) or \\(i=k\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  Hence **\\(L_1\\) and \\(L_k\\) cannot pass through \\((n,1)\\)**; all other lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) may (and will) contain \\((n,1)\\) in order to cover the rightmost point.\n\n   *Step 5 – interior columns must be occupied by distinct lines.*  \n   The interior columns of \\(S_k\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   a=n-k+2,\\;n-k+3,\\dots ,n-1,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. exactly \\(k-2\\) columns.  Consider any two distinct sunny lines \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  They already intersect in the leftmost column at distinct points, and (for \\(2\\le i,j\\le k-1\\)) they also intersect in the rightmost column at the common point \\((n,1)\\).  Two distinct lines can share **at most one** point; therefore two different lines among \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) cannot meet the same interior column, otherwise they would have two common points (the leftmost point and the interior point).  Consequently each interior column is intersected by **exactly one** of the lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\).\n\n   The two extreme lines \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_k\\) do not contain \\((n,1)\\); each of them must intersect the triangle in some interior column.  If they intersect the same interior column, they would again share two points (their leftmost points and the interior point), contradicting the fact that distinct lines intersect at most once.  Hence \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_k\\) occupy **two different** interior columns.\n\n   Altogether the \\(k\\) lines occupy the \\(k-2\\) interior columns in a one‑to‑one fashion, leaving **no interior column free**.\n\n   *Step 6 – a column with two points remains uncovered – contradiction.*  \n   The leftmost interior column is \\(a=n-k+2\\); it contains the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k+2,1),\\qquad (n-k+2,2).\n   \\]\n\n   Exactly one of the sunny lines meets this column (by the one‑to‑one correspondence of Step 5).  Hence at most one of the two points can be covered, and the other point stays uncovered.  This contradicts the requirement that **all** points of \\(T_n\\) be covered.\n\n   Therefore a configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only values of \\(k\\) for which a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions exists are  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0,\\qquad k=1,\\qquad k=3 .\n   \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
