            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. it has one of the three forms  \n\n   \\[\n   x=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Explicit constructions** for the three values that turn out to be possible.  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\),\n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y-1=\\frac{1}{1-n}\\,(x-n)\\qquad\\Bigl(\\text{slope }-\\frac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); the unique point with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(L\\).  Thus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  For any \\(n\\ge3\\) use the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: \\text{through }(n-2,1)\\text{ and }(n-1,2) &&\\text{slope }+1,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_2 &: \\text{through }(n-2,2)\\text{ and }(n,1)   &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_3 &: \\text{through }(n-2,3)\\text{ and }(n-1,1) &&\\text{slope }-2,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   together with the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\n   \\]\n   (the set of vertical lines is empty when \\(n=3\\)).  \n\n   **Verification of the six right‑most points.**  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\n   \\]\n\n   is covered as follows (substitutions are shown).\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{line containing it} & \\text{check} \\\\ \\hline\n   (n-2,1) & L_1 & 1 = 1\\cdot (n-2)+(-\\,n+3)\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-1,2) & L_1 & 2 = 1\\cdot (n-1)+(-\\,n+3)\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-2,2) & L_2 & 2 = -\\tfrac12 (n-2)+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n,1)   & L_2 & 1 = -\\tfrac12 n+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-2,3) & L_3 & 3 = -2 (n-2)+(2n-1)\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-1,1) & L_3 & 1 = -2 (n-1)+(2n-1)\\;\\checkmark\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Every other point of \\(T_n\\) lies in a column \\(a\\le n-3\\) and is therefore on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  The three slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) are none of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence exactly three lines are sunny.\n\n3. **A reduction lemma** (used in the impossibility proofs).  \n\n   *Lemma.*  Let a configuration contain a non‑sunny line that is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  Replace it by the vertical line \\(x=a\\) where \\(a\\) is the (unique) column of a point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on the original line.  The new configuration still covers every point that was covered before.\n\n   *Proof.*  In any fixed column \\(a\\) the set \\(T_n\\) contains at most one point with a given \\(y\\)-coordinate, and also at most one point with a given value of \\(x+y\\).  Hence a horizontal line or a line of slope \\(-1\\) meets each column in **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).  The vertical line \\(x=a\\) meets the same column in **all** points of that column, in particular the point that was on the original line.  Therefore no point of \\(T_n\\) is lost by the replacement. ∎\n\n   Consequently, when we study the existence of a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we may, without loss of generality, assume that all \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are vertical.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n\n   After the reduction we have \\(n-2\\) vertical lines covering the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n   The only points of \\(T_n\\) that may remain uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   P=\\{(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Any line that contains a point of column \\(n-1\\) and the point \\((n,1)\\) is either\n\n   * vertical – impossible because the line would have to be \\(x=n-1\\);\n   * horizontal – the line through \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\);\n   * of slope \\(-1\\) – the line through \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).\n\n   All three possibilities are **non‑sunny**.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of the set \\(P\\).  With exactly two sunny lines we can cover at most two points of \\(P\\), leaving at least one point uncovered – a contradiction.  Therefore no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   Assume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   By the reduction lemma we may take the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines cover all points of the columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-k\\).  \n   The uncovered points form the right‑hand triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n   \\]\n   which is a translate of \\(T_k\\) and therefore contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\]\n   points.\n\n   *Column \\(n-k+1\\).*  \n   This leftmost column of the triangle contains exactly \\(k\\) points.  \n   Because a sunny line is **not** vertical, it can meet this column in at most one point.  Consequently each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain a **different** point of this column; denote by \\(L_i\\) the line that contains \\((n-k+1,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).\n\n   *Column \\(n\\) (the rightmost column).*  \n   It consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\).  At most one of the lines \\(L_i\\) can pass through this point, because any line that contains \\((n,1)\\) together with a point of the leftmost column has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1-i}{k-1},\n   \\]\n   which is forbidden precisely when \\(i=1\\) (slope \\(0\\)) or \\(i=k\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  Hence **only the lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) are capable of passing through \\((n,1)\\)**, and at most one of them actually does.\n\n   *Interior columns.*  \n   The interior columns are  \n\n   \\[\n   a=n-k+2,\\;n-k+3,\\dots ,n-1,\n   \\]\n   i.e. exactly \\(k-2\\) columns.  \n   Suppose two distinct lines \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) (\\(i\\neq j\\)) both contain a point of the same interior column.  They already contain distinct points of the leftmost column, so they would share **two** distinct points, which is impossible for two different lines.  Therefore **each interior column can contain points of at most one sunny line**.\n\n   We have \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   One of them (if any) may go through \\((n,1)\\); the remaining \\(k-1\\) lines must each meet a distinct interior column, because otherwise two of them would share an interior column, contradicting the previous paragraph.  Hence we need at least \\(k-1\\) distinct interior columns.\n\n   However there are only