            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. it has one of the three forms  \n\n   \\[\n   x=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines.**  \n   *Lemma (exchange).*  \n   Let a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   If a non‑sunny line \\(L\\) is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)), then we can replace \\(L\\) by a vertical line \\(x=a\\) (where \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\cap L\\)) without losing any covered point and without creating a duplicate line.  \n   *Proof.*  \n   The set \\(C:=T_n\\cap L\\) consists of points \\((a_i,b_i)\\) with distinct abscissae \\(a_i\\) (because \\(L\\) is not vertical). Choose one of these abscissae, say \\(a_0\\), that is not already used by another vertical line; such an \\(a_0\\) exists unless every column containing a point of \\(C\\) already has a vertical line, in which case all points of \\(C\\) are already covered and we may simply delete \\(L\\) and later add a line that does not meet \\(T_n\\) (e.g. \\(x=-1000\\)).  \n   Replacing \\(L\\) by the vertical line \\(x=a_0\\) certainly covers the point \\((a_0,b_0)\\in C\\) and may cover additional points of the same column, so no point of \\(T_n\\) is lost. ∎  \n\n   Repeating the exchange eliminates all horizontal and slope‑\\(-1\\) non‑sunny lines. Hence **without loss of generality we may assume that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are vertical**.\n\n3. **Explicit constructions** for the three values that turn out to be possible.  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\),\n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y-1=\\frac{1}{1-n}\\,(x-n)\\qquad\\Bigl(\\text{slope }-\\frac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   - If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\(x=a\\) is one of the vertical lines, so the point is covered.  \n   - The unique point with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\); it satisfies the equation of \\(L\\) because substituting \\(x=n\\) gives \\(y=1\\).  \n   Thus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  For any \\(n\\ge3\\) use the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y =  \\;x   - (n-3) &&\\text{slope }+1,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x +\\tfrac{n}{2}+1 &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -2x + (2n-1) &&\\text{slope }-2,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   together with the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\n   \\]\n   (the set of vertical lines is empty when \\(n=3\\)).  \n\n   *Verification of the six right‑most points.*  \n   The points that are not in the columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-3\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting each point into the equation of the claimed line gives:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{line containing it} & \\text{check} \\\\ \\hline\n   (n-2,1) & L_1 & 1 = (n-2)-(n-3)=1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-1,2) & L_1 & 2 = (n-1)-(n-3)=2\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-2,2) & L_2 & 2 = -\\tfrac12 (n-2)+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n,1)   & L_2 & 1 = -\\tfrac12 n+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-2,3) & L_3 & 3 = -2 (n-2)+(2n-1)\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-1,1) & L_3 & 1 = -2 (n-1)+(2n-1)\\;\\checkmark\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Every other point of \\(T_n\\) lies in a column \\(a\\le n-3\\) and is therefore on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   The three slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) are none of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence exactly three lines are sunny.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n\n   By the exchange lemma we may assume the two non‑sunny lines are vertical, say \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  \n   These cover all points of the columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n   The uncovered points form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   P=\\{(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Any line that contains two of the points of \\(P\\) is either horizontal \\((y=1)\\), vertical \\((x=n-1)\\) or of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=n+1)\\); all three possibilities are non‑sunny.  \n   Consequently each sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(P\\).  \n   With exactly two sunny lines we can cover at most two points of \\(P\\), leaving at least one point uncovered – a contradiction.  \n   Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   Assume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   By the exchange lemma we may take the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   These cover all points of the columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-k\\).  \n   The remaining points form the right‑hand triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n   \\]\n   which is a translate of \\(T_k\\) and therefore contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\]\n   points.\n\n   **(i) Leftmost column.**  \n   Column \\(c_0=n-k+1\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) points \\((c_0,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n   Because a sunny line is not vertical, it can meet this column in at most one point.  \n   Hence each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain a *different* point of this column; denote by \\(L_i\\) the line that contains \\((c_0,i)\\).\n\n   **(ii) Rightmost column.**  \n   Column \\(n\\) consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   For a line \\(L_i\\) the slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-i}{k-1}\\).  \n   This slope equals \\(0\\) when \\(i=1\\) and equals \\(-1\\) when \\(i=k\\); both are forbidden for a sunny line.  \n   Thus \\(L_i\\) can pass through \\((n,1)\\) only when \\(2\\le i\\le k-1\\).  \n   Since the lines are distinct, at most **one** of the \\(k\\) lines can actually contain \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Consequently at least \\(k-1\\) of the points \\((c_0,i)\\) (those with \\(i\\neq\\) the index of the possible line through \\((n,1)\\)) must be matched with points in the *interior* columns \\(c_0+1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\n   **(iii) Interior columns.**  \n   There are exactly \\(k-2\\) interior columns.  \n   Suppose two different sunny lines \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) (\\(i\\neq j\\)) both contain a point of the same interior column \\(a\\).  \n   Then the two lines share two distinct points: \\((c_0,i)\\) and a point in column \\(a\\).  \n   Two distinct points determine a unique line, so the two lines would coincide, contradicting their distinctness.  \n   Hence **each interior column can contain points of at most one sunny line**.\n\n   We have \\(k-1\\) lines that must be assigned to interior columns (all except possibly the one that goes through \\((n,1)\\)).  \n   Because there are only \\(k-2\\) interior columns, this is impossible when \\(k\\ge4\\).  \n   Therefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines can exist.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only values of \\(k\\) for which a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions exists are  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0,\\qquad k=1,\\qquad k=3 .\n   \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
