 sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\):  \n> \\(y-1=\\frac{1}{1-n}(x-n)\\) (slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n> Verification: points with \\(a\\le n-1\\) are covered by a vertical line; the unique point with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\) and lies on the sunny line.”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\((2-1)/(1-n)=1/(1-n)=-1/(n-1)\\), which is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for any \\(n\\ge3\\); thus it is sunny. All points with \\(a\\le n-1\\) are covered by the corresponding vertical line, and \\((n,1)\\) is covered by the sunny line. **Correct.**\n\n---\n\n### 6. Construction for \\(k=3\\)  \n\n> “For any \\(n\\ge3\\) use three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> L_1:y=x-(n-3),\\qquad\n> L_2:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac n2+1,\\qquad\n> L_3:y=-2x+(2n-1),\n> \\]  \n> together with the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) (empty when \\(n=3\\)).  \n> Verification of the six right‑most points \\(S=\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\\) is given in a table; every other point lies in a column \\(\\le n-3\\) and is covered by a vertical line. The three slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which are forbidden, so exactly three lines are sunny.”\n\n**Verification:**  \nDirect substitution shows each listed point satisfies the equation of the claimed line (the table is correct). All remaining points have first coordinate \\(\\le n-3\\) and are covered by the corresponding vertical line. The slopes are indeed \\(1,-\\frac12,-2\\), none equal to \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). Hence the construction works and yields exactly three sunny lines. **Correct.**\n\n---\n\n### 7. Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)  \n\n> “Assume the two non‑sunny lines are vertical \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  \n> The uncovered points are \\(P=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\\).  \n> Any line containing two of these points is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(P\\). With exactly two sunny lines we can cover at most two points of \\(P\\), leaving one uncovered – a contradiction. Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe three points indeed form a “L‑shape” where any pair determines a line of forbidden slope (horizontal, vertical, or \\(-1\\)). Consequently each sunny line can cover at most one of them, and two sunny lines cannot cover all three. The argument is sound. **Correct.**\n\n---\n\n### 8. Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\) – **Critical Error**\n\n> **(i) Leftmost column.** “Column \\(c_0=n-k+1\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) points \\((c_0,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\). Since a sunny line is not vertical, it can meet this column in at most one point. Hence each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain a different point of this column; denote by \\(L_i\\) the line that contains \\((c_0,i)\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \nCorrect: each sunny line intersects a given vertical line in at most one point, and there are exactly \\(k\\) points to be covered, so each sunny line must take a distinct one.\n\n> **(ii) Rightmost column.** “Column \\(n\\) consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\). For a line \\(L_i\\) the slope is \\(\\frac{1-i}{k-1}\\). This slope equals \\(0\\) when \\(i=1\\) and equals \\(-1\\) when \\(i=k\\); both are forbidden for a sunny line. Thus \\(L_i\\) can pass through \\((n,1)\\) only when \\(2\\le i\\le k-1\\). Since the lines are distinct, at most one of the \\(k\\) lines can actually contain \\((n,1)\\). Consequently at least \\(k-1\\) of the points \\((c_0,i)\\) … must be matched with points in the interior columns \\(c_0+1,\\dots ,n-1\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe slope computation is correct; the conclusion that at most one sunny line can contain \\((n,1)\\) follows. Hence at least \\(k-1\\) sunny lines must meet the interior columns. **Correct.**\n\n> **(iii) Interior columns.** “There are exactly \\(k-2\\) interior columns. Suppose two different sunny lines \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) (\\(i\\neq j\\)) both contain a point of the same interior column \\(a\\). Then the two lines share two distinct points: \\((c_0,i)\\) and a point in column \\(a\\). Two distinct points determine a unique line, so the two lines would coincide, contradicting their distinctness. Hence each interior column can contain points of at most one sunny line.”\n\n**Verification – **Critical Error**:**  \nThe argument is false. Two distinct lines can intersect the same vertical column at *different* points; they would then share only the point \\((c_0,i)\\) (or \\((c_0,j)\\)), not a second common point. The statement “the two lines share two distinct points” is incorrect unless the two lines happen to pass through the *same* point of the interior column, which is not required. Consequently the conclusion “each interior column can contain points of at most one sunny line” is unjustified and false.\n\nA concrete counterexample (with \\(n=4,\\;k=4\\)) demonstrates the flaw:\n\n*Take the four sunny lines*\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: (1,1)\\text{–}(2,2) \\;(y=x),\\\\\nL_2 &: (1,2)\\text{–}(3,1) \\;(y=-\\tfrac12x+ \\tfrac52),\\\\\nL_3 &: (1,3)\\text{–}(4,1) \\;(y=-\\tfrac23x+\\tfrac{11}{3}),\\\\\nL_4 &: (1,4)\\text{–}(2,1) \\;(y=-3x+7),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhich are all sunny (slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac23,-3\\)).  \nColumns \\(2\\) and \\(3\\) each contain points belonging to *different* sunny lines (e.g. column 2 contains points of \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_4\\)). Thus an interior column can host points of several sunny lines, contradicting the claim.\n\nBecause the claim in (iii) is false, the subsequent counting argument (“\\(k-1\\) lines must be assigned to only \\(k-2\\) interior columns”) collapses. Hence the proof that no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) exists is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Consequence for the final answer  \n\nThe erroneous impossibility proof leads to the incorrect conclusion that the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\). In fact, configurations with \\(k\\ge4\\) exist (e.g. the explicit construction for \\(n=4,\\;k=4\\) above), so the answer is incomplete and false.\n\n---\n\n**Overall assessment:** The solution is invalid because of a critical logical error in the impossibility argument for \\(k\\ge4\\). The error invalidates the final answer."
