ertical lines is empty when \\(n=3\\)).\n\n   **Verification of the six points not lying in the first \\(n-3\\) columns.**  \n   The uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting each point into the equation of the claimed line gives:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{line containing it} & \\text{check} \\\\ \\hline\n   (n-2,1) & L_1 & 1=(n-2)-(n-3)=1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-1,2) & L_1 & 2=(n-1)-(n-3)=2\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-2,2) & L_2 & 2=-\\tfrac12 (n-2)+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n,1)   & L_2 & 1=-\\tfrac12 n+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-2,3) & L_3 & 3=-2 (n-2)+(2n-1)\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-1,1) & L_3 & 1=-2 (n-1)+(2n-1)\\;\\checkmark\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Every other point of \\(T_n\\) lies in a column \\(a\\le n-3\\) and is therefore on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   The three slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) are none of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence exactly three lines are sunny.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 4\\).**  \n\n   Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   By the Exchange Lemma we may suppose that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   After removing those columns, the remaining points form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n   \\]\n   which is a translate of \\(T_k\\) and therefore contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\]\n   points.\n\n   **(i) The leftmost column.**  \n   Column \\(c_0=n-k+1\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) points \\((c_0,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n   A non‑vertical line meets this column in at most one point, so each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain a *different* point of this column; denote by \\(L_i\\) the line that contains \\((c_0,i)\\).\n\n   **(ii) The rightmost column.**  \n   Column \\(n\\) consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   For \\(i=1\\) the line \\(L_1\\) would have slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), which is forbidden; for \\(i=k\\) the line \\(L_k\\) would have slope \\(-1\\), also forbidden.  \n   Consequently at most the lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) could pass through \\((n,1)\\).  \n   If two distinct lines among them both passed through \\((n,1)\\) they would coincide, contradicting distinctness. Hence **at most one** of the \\(k\\) sunny lines can contain the point \\((n,1)\\).\n\n   **(iii) Interior columns.**  \n   The columns \\(c_0+1,\\dots ,n-1\\) are exactly \\(k-2\\) in number.  \n   Each of the remaining \\(k-1\\) lines (all except possibly the one that goes through \\((n,1)\\)) must meet the leftmost column at a point different from \\((c_0,1)\\) and \\((c_0,k)\\); therefore they must meet the interior columns in order to cover the points of \\(S_k\\) that are not already covered.  \n   A single line can intersect a given interior column in at most one point, so the total number of incidences that the \\(k-1\\) lines can provide inside the interior columns is at most \\((k-1)(k-2)\\).\n\n   **(iv) Counting the required incidences.**  \n   The set \\(S_k\\) contains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.  \n   We have already accounted for the \\(k\\) points of the leftmost column and the single point of the rightmost column, i.e. \\(k+1\\) points.  \n   Hence the interior columns must contain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2-(k+1)=\\frac{(k-2)(k+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points that still need to be covered.\n\n   For \\(k\\ge4\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(k-2)(k+1)}2 > (k-1)(k-2),\n   \\]\n\n   because the inequality is equivalent to \\(k+1>2k-2\\), i.e. \\(3>k\\), which fails when \\(k\\ge4\\).  \n   Thus the interior columns contain **more** points than the maximal number of incidences that the \\(k-1\\) remaining sunny lines can supply, a contradiction.\n\n   Therefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only values of \\(k\\) for which a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions exists are  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=2,\\;k=3 .\n   \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\;}\n\\] ∎"
