            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. it has one of the three forms  \n\n   \\[\n   x=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Exchange Lemma.**  \n   *Lemma.*  \n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   If \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a non‑sunny line that is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)), then we can replace that line by a vertical line \\(x=a\\) (where \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the original line) without losing any covered point and without creating a duplicate line.  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The set \\(C:=T_n\\cap L\\) contains points with pairwise different abscissae because \\(L\\) is not vertical. Choose an abscissa \\(a\\) that is not already used by another vertical line; such an \\(a\\) exists unless every column containing a point of \\(C\\) already has a vertical line, in which case all points of \\(C\\) are already covered and we may simply delete \\(L\\). Replacing \\(L\\) by the vertical line \\(x=a\\) certainly covers the point \\((a,b)\\in C\\) and possibly additional points of the same column, so no point of \\(T_n\\) is lost. ∎  \n\n   Repeating the exchange eliminates all horizontal and slope‑\\(-1\\) non‑sunny lines. Consequently **we may assume that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are vertical**:\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Explicit constructions** for the values that turn out to be possible.\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence lies on the line \\(x=a\\). All lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-2,2)\\),\n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y-1=\\frac{1}{\\,2-n\\,}(x-n)\\qquad\\Bigl(\\text{slope }-\\frac1{\\,n-2\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   **Verification.**  \n   - If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\(x=a\\) is one of the vertical lines, so the point is covered.  \n   - The unique point with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\); it satisfies the equation of \\(L\\).  \n\n   Thus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  \n   Use the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2\n   \\]\n\n   and the two sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y-1=-\\tfrac12\\bigl(x-(n-2)\\bigr) &&\\text{(passes through }(n-2,2)\\text{ and }(n,1)),\\\\[2mm]\n   L_2 &: y-1=2\\bigl(x-(n-1)\\bigr)   &&\\text{(passes through }(n-1,1)\\text{ only)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   **Verification.**  \n   - Columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-2\\) are covered by the vertical lines.  \n   - \\((n-2,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on \\(L_1\\) (substituting \\(x=n-2\\) gives \\(y=2\\); substituting \\(x=n\\) gives \\(y=1\\)).  \n   - \\((n-1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_2\\) (substituting \\(x=n-1\\) gives \\(y=1\\)).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly two lines are sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Use the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y =  x-(n-3) &&\\text{slope }+1,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x +\\tfrac{n}{2}+1 &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -2x + (2n-1) &&\\text{slope }-2,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   together with the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\n   \\]\n   (the set of vertical lines is empty when \\(n=3\\)).\n\n   **Verification of the six points not lying in the first \\(n-3\\) columns.**  \n   The uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting each point into the equation of the claimed line gives:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{line containing it} & \\text{check} \\\\ \\hline\n   (n-2,1) & L_1 & 1=(n-2)-(n-3)=1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-1,2) & L_1 & 2=(n-1)-(n-3)=2\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-2,2) & L_2 & 2=-\\tfrac12 (n-2)+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n,1)   & L_2 & 1=-\\tfrac12 n+\\tfrac{n}{2}+1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-2,3) & L_3 & 3=-2 (n-2)+(2n-1)\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (n-1,1) & L_3 & 1=-2 (n-1)+(2n-1)\\;\\checkmark\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Every other point of \\(T_n\\) lies in a column \\(a\\le n-3\\) and is therefore on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   The three slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) are none of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence exactly three lines are sunny.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 4\\).**  \n\n   Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   By the Exchange Lemma we may suppose that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   After removing those columns, the remaining points form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n   \\]\n   which is a translate of \\(T_k\\) and therefore contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\]\n   points.\n\n   **(i) The leftmost column.**  \n   Column \\(c_0=n-k+1\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) points \\((c_0,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n   A non‑vertical line meets this column in at most one point, so each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain a *different* point of this column; denote by \\(L_i\\) the line that contains \\((c_0,i)\\).\n\n   **(ii) The rightmost column.**  \n   Column \\(n\\) consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   For \\(i=1\\) the line \\(L_1\\) would have slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), which is forbidden; for \\(i=k\\) the line \\(L_k\\) would have slope \\(-1\\), also forbidden.  \n   Consequently at most the lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) could pass through \\((n,1)\\).  \n   If two distinct lines among them both passed through \\((n,1)\\) they would coincide, contradicting distinctness. Hence **at most one** of the \\(k\\) sunny lines can contain the point \\((n,1)\\).\n\n   **(iii) Interior columns.**  \n   The columns \\(c_0+1,\\dots ,n-1\\) are exactly \\(k-2\\) in number.  \n   Each of the remaining \\(k-1\\) lines (all except possibly the one that goes through \\((n,1)\\)) must meet the leftmost column at a point different from \\((c_0,1)\\) and \\((c_0,k)\\); therefore they must meet the interior columns in order to cover the points of \\(S_k\\) that are not already covered.  \n   A single line can intersect a given interior column in at most one point, so the total number of incidences that the \\(k-1\\) lines can provide inside the interior columns is at most \\((k-1)(k-2)\\).\n\n   **(iv) Counting the required incidences.**  \n   The set \\(S_k\\) contains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.  \n   We have already accounted for the \\(k\\) points of the leftmost column and the single point of the rightmost column, i.e. \\(k+1\\) points.  \n   Hence the interior columns must contain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2-(k+1)=\\frac{(k-2)(k+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points that still need to be covered.\n\n   For \\(k\\ge4\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(k-2)(k+1)}2 > (k-1)(k-2),\n   \\]\n\n   because the inequality is equivalent to \\(k+1>2k-2\\), i.e. \\(3>k\\), which fails when \\(k\\ge4\\).  \n   Thus the interior columns contain **more** points than the maximal number of incidences that the \\(k-1\\) remaining sunny lines can supply, a contradiction.\n\n   Therefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only values of \\(k\\) for which a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions exists are  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=2,\\;k=3 .\n   \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\;}\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
