\n\n---\n\n##### 3.4  \\(k=3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_3=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\\,\\}\\cup\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\},\n\\]\nwith  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: \\text{ line through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: \\text{ line through }(n-1,2)\\text{ and }(n-2,1),\\\\\nL_3 &: \\text{ line through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExplicit equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-1=-\\frac12\\,(x-n),\\\\\nL_2 &: y-2= 1\\,(x-(n-1)),\\\\\nL_3 &: y-1=-2\\,(x-(n-1)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*\n\nThe six points that are not in the first \\(n-3\\) columns are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(n,1)      & L_1\\\\\n(n-1,1)    & L_3\\\\\n(n-1,2)    & L_2\\\\\n(n-2,1)    & L_2\\\\\n(n-2,2)    & L_1\\\\\n(n-2,3)    & L_3\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nDirect substitution confirms each entry (e.g. for \\((n-2,2)\\):  \n\\(y-1=-\\frac12\\,(n-2-n) = -\\frac12(-2)=1\\Rightarrow y=2\\)).  \n\nAll points with abscissa \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\) lie on the corresponding vertical line.  \n\nThe slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,\\;1,\\;-2\\) are none of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the three lines are sunny.  Exactly three sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Impossibility for \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists for some \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nBy Corollary 2 we may suppose that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are vertical; denote their columns by  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_{\\,n-k}\\}\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus V\n\\]\n\nbe the set of columns **without** a vertical line; \\(|R|=k\\).\nRe‑ordering the columns (which does not affect the combinatorial argument) we may assume  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{\\,n-k+1,\\;n-k+2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a column \\(a\\in R\\) the admissible points are  \n\n\\[\n(a,1),\\;(a,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(a,\\,n+1-a\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nHence the points that are not already covered by a vertical line form the triangular set  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_k=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\},\n\\qquad |\\Delta_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(\\Delta_k\\) lying on the anti‑diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\) are  \n\n\\[\nD_k=\\{(n-k+i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Claim 1.** *No sunny line can contain two points of \\(D_k\\).*  \nIndeed, for \\((n-k+i,i)\\) and \\((n-k+j,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) the slope equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{j-i}{(n-k+j)-(n-k+i)}=\\frac{j-i}{j-i}=-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is forbidden.  Hence each point of \\(D_k\\) lies on a distinct sunny line.\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines are exactly the lines that contain the points of \\(D_k\\); denote them by \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) with  \n\n\\[\nL_i\\supseteq (n-k+i,i).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Claim 2.** *For every \\(i\\) the line \\(L_i\\) cannot contain any other point of column \\(n-k+i\\).*  \nIf \\(L_i\\) contained a second point \\((n-k+i,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq i\\), the line would have two points with the same abscissa, i.e. it would be vertical, contradicting the fact that \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThus, after the points of \\(D_k\\) are covered, the remaining points of \\(\\Delta_k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_k\\setminus D_k\n   =\\{(n-k+i,b)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\nwhose cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2-k=\\frac{k(k-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nEach of these points lies in a column already occupied by the anti‑diagonal point of the same column, and by Claim 2 the line that already passes through that column’s anti‑diagonal point cannot meet the column again.  Hence **every** point of \\(\\Delta_k\\setminus D_k\\) must be covered by a sunny line *different* from the line that contains its column’s anti‑diagonal point.\n\nTherefore we need at least  \n\n\\[\nk\\;+\\;\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\;=\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\]\n\ndistinct sunny lines to cover all points of \\(\\Delta_k\\).  Since the configuration contains only \\(k\\) sunny lines, we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 3 .\n\\]\n\nThis contradicts the assumption \\(k\\ge4\\).  Hence no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  The special case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe argument of Section 4 with \\(k=2\\) gives the lower bound \\(\\frac{2\\cdot3}{2}=3\\) sunny lines, so \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  \nThe constructions of Sections 3.1, 3.2 and 3.4 give feasible families for \\(k=0,1,3\\).  Hence for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n* For the smallest case \\(n=3\\) the admissible numbers are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth statements are proved by the explicit constructions of Section 3 and the impossibility proof of Section 4. ∎"
