"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n(For the special case \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible; for all \\(n\\ge4\\) the four values above are attainable.)\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to vertical non\u2011sunny lines** \u2013 an exchange lemma shows that any configuration can be transformed, without changing the number of sunny lines, into a configuration in which every non\u2011sunny line is vertical.  \n2. **Explicit constructions** \u2013 for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) we give a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) is checked line\u2011by\u2011line.  \n3. **Impossibility for \\(k\\ge4\\)** \u2013 after the reduction, the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines occupy \\(n-k\\) distinct columns.  \n   The remaining \\(k\\) columns are the right\u2011most ones \\(\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); together they contain the triangular set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n   \\qquad |\\Delta_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   In \\(\\Delta_k\\) the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) form the anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D_k=\\{(n-k+i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *No sunny line can contain two points of \\(D_k\\)* (the slope would be \\(-1\\)), and a sunny line cannot contain two points of the same column (otherwise it would be vertical).  \n   Consequently each point of \\(D_k\\) must lie on a **different** sunny line; thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines are exactly the lines that contain the points of \\(D_k\\).  \n\n   Consider the remaining points of \\(\\Delta_k\\); there are  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\Delta_k|-|D_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2-k=\\frac{k(k-1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   of them.  Every such point lies in a column that already contains a point of \\(D_k\\); a sunny line that already contains the column\u2019s anti\u2011diagonal point cannot meet the same column again (otherwise it would be vertical).  Hence each of the \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\) points must be covered by a **different** sunny line from the one that covers its column\u2019s anti\u2011diagonal point.  \n\n   Therefore at least  \n\n   \\[\n   k+\\frac{k(k-1)}2=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   distinct sunny lines are required.  Since we have only \\(k\\) sunny lines, we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le 3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   The argument also shows that for \\(n=3\\) the case \\(k=2\\) is impossible (the triangular set \\(\\Delta_2\\) already forces three distinct sunny lines), while for every \\(n\\ge4\\) the constructions of step\u202f2 realise \\(k=2\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  Notation  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThe three families of non\u2011sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nx=c\\;( \\text{vertical}),\\qquad y=c\\;( \\text{horizontal}),\\qquad x+y=c\\;( \\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.  Exchange Lemma  \n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n> If \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a non\u2011sunny line that is not vertical, then one can replace that line by a vertical line \\(x=a\\) (with \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)) so that the new family still covers \\(T_n\\) and still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume the non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny line is \\(L\\).  \nBecause \\(L\\) is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)), the set  \n\n\\[\nC:=T_n\\cap L\n\\]\n\ncontains at least one point; all points of \\(C\\) have pairwise different abscissae (otherwise two points would have the same \\(x\\), forcing \\(L\\) to be vertical).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nA:=\\{a\\mid (a,b)\\in C\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of those abscissae.  \nThe family already contains at most \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, where \\(k\\) is the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nHence at most \\(n-k\\) integers of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are used as columns of vertical lines.  \nSince \\(|A|\\ge 1\\) and \\(|\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus A|\\ge k\\ge 1\\), we can choose an integer \\(a\\) that is **not** the abscissa of any existing vertical line and also not in \\(A\\).  \nReplace \\(L\\) by the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nThe point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(b\\) minimal (namely \\(b=1\\)) is now covered by the new line; every other point of \\(C\\) remains covered because its column belongs to \\(A\\) and those columns are still covered by the original vertical lines (if any) or by other non\u2011vertical lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nDistinctness is preserved because the new column \\(a\\) was unused. \u220e\n\n\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f1 repeatedly we obtain:\n\n> **Corollary 2.**  \n> For any feasible configuration there exists a feasible configuration with the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in which **all** non\u2011sunny lines are vertical.  Consequently the set of vertical lines is of the form  \n\n> \\[\n> x=c_1,\\;x=c_2,\\dots ,x=c_{\\,n-k},\n> \\qquad c_1,\\dots ,c_{\\,n-k}\\text{ distinct elements of }\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n> \\]\n\n#### 3.  Explicit constructions  \n\nBelow we list, for each admissible \\(k\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nAll verifications are performed point\u2011by\u2011point.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll lines are vertical, therefore non\u2011sunny.  Exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{L\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(L\\) is the line through the two points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\):\n\\[\nL:\\;y-1=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n).\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n* Columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\): each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\n* Column \\(n\\): the only point is \\((n,1)\\); substituting \\(x=n\\) in the equation of \\(L\\) gives \\(y=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\in L\\).  \n\nThe slope of \\(L\\) equals \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(L\\) is sunny.  Exactly one sunny line.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.3  \\(k=2\\)   (valid for every \\(n\\ge4\\); the case \\(n=3\\) is impossible, see Section\u202f5)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_2=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\;y=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{L_1,L_2\\},\n\\]\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: \\text{ line through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(n-1,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: \\text{ line through }(1,3)\\text{ and }(n-2,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExplicit equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-1=\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}(x-1),\\\\[2mm]\nL_2 &: y-3=-\\frac{2}{\\,n-3\\,}(x-1)\\qquad (n\\ge4).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*\n\n* Columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-2\\): covered by the vertical lines.  \n\n* Row \\(y=1\\): the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) covers all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge n-1\\), i.e. \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \n\n* Point \\((n-1,2)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\) (substitute \\(x=n-1\\) gives \\(y=2\\)).  \n\n* Points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) lie on \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) respectively (by construction).  \n\n* Point \\((n-2,1)\\) lies on \\(L_2\\) (substitute \\(x=n-2\\) gives \\(y=1\\)).  \n\nAll remaining points belong to a column already covered by a vertical line, hence are covered.  \n\nThe slopes \\(\\frac1{n-2}\\) and \\(-\\frac2{n-3}\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore both \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are sunny.  Exactly two sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.4  \\(k=3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_3=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3\\,\\}\\cup\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\},\n\\]\nwith  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: \\text{ line through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: \\text{ line through }(n-1,2)\\text{ and }(n-2,1),\\\\\nL_3 &: \\text{ line through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExplicit equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-1=-\\frac12\\,(x-n),\\\\\nL_2 &: y-2= 1\\,(x-(n-1)),\\\\\nL_3 &: y-1=-2\\,(x-(n-1)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*\n\nThe six points that are not in the first \\(n-3\\) columns are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(n,1)      & L_1\\\\\n(n-1,1)    & L_3\\\\\n(n-1,2)    & L_2\\\\\n(n-2,1)    & L_2\\\\\n(n-2,2)    & L_1\\\\\n(n-2,3)    & L_3\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nDirect substitution confirms each entry (e.g. for \\((n-2,2)\\):  \n\\(y-1=-\\frac12\\,(n-2-n) = -\\frac12(-2)=1\\Rightarrow y=2\\)).  \n\nAll points with abscissa \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\) lie on the corresponding vertical line.  \n\nThe slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,\\;1,\\;-2\\) are none of \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the three lines are sunny.  Exactly three sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Impossibility for \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists for some \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nBy Corollary\u202f2 we may suppose that the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are vertical; denote their columns by  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_{\\,n-k}\\}\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus V\n\\]\n\nbe the set of columns **without** a vertical line; \\(|R|=k\\).\nRe\u2011ordering the columns (which does not affect the combinatorial argument) we may assume  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{\\,n-k+1,\\;n-k+2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a column \\(a\\in R\\) the admissible points are  \n\n\\[\n(a,1),\\;(a,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(a,\\,n+1-a\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nHence the points that are not already covered by a vertical line form the triangular set  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_k=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\},\n\\qquad |\\Delta_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(\\Delta_k\\) lying on the anti\u2011diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\) are  \n\n\\[\nD_k=\\{(n-k+i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Claim 1.** *No sunny line can contain two points of \\(D_k\\).*  \nIndeed, for \\((n-k+i,i)\\) and \\((n-k+j,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) the slope equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{j-i}{(n-k+j)-(n-k+i)}=\\frac{j-i}{j-i}=-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is forbidden.  Hence each point of \\(D_k\\) lies on a distinct sunny line.\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines are exactly the lines that contain the points of \\(D_k\\); denote them by \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) with  \n\n\\[\nL_i\\supseteq (n-k+i,i).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Claim 2.** *For every \\(i\\) the line \\(L_i\\) cannot contain any other point of column \\(n-k+i\\).*  \nIf \\(L_i\\) contained a second point \\((n-k+i,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq i\\), the line would have two points with the same abscissa, i.e. it would be vertical, contradicting the fact that \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nThus, after the points of \\(D_k\\) are covered, the remaining points of \\(\\Delta_k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_k\\setminus D_k\n   =\\{(n-k+i,b)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\nwhose cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2-k=\\frac{k(k-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nEach of these points lies in a column already occupied by the anti\u2011diagonal point of the same column, and by Claim\u202f2 the line that already passes through that column\u2019s anti\u2011diagonal point cannot meet the column again.  Hence **every** point of \\(\\Delta_k\\setminus D_k\\) must be covered by a sunny line *different* from the line that contains its column\u2019s anti\u2011diagonal point.\n\nTherefore we need at least  \n\n\\[\nk\\;+\\;\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\;=\\;\\frac{k(